{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The specified durable record is purely meta-temporal—causal timestamps and a happens-before proof—and omits any content-addressed reference to the completed evidence itself. Consequently the protocol can satisfy its own record-keeping requirement while the actual evidence is silently displaced from the reclaimed workspace. Moreover, because the crashed holder leaves no terminal record behind, the seal-emission timestamp and happens-before proof must reside in an external causal store that the target never identifies or justifies; the answer therefore evades the requirement to determine a concrete safest protocol by offering only an ungrounded comparative latency criterion.","target":"090a8fee07ca4d3156adba977154197fc20207f3d956711a724d6094f7d604f4"},{"attack":true,"case":"The target claims dominance over lease-based rivals because the log 'records completion before reclamation can observe death,' but this temporal ordering is equally achievable by any correctly implemented lease protocol (the holder commits its seal to durable storage before the lease expires). Because the adjudication relies on an invariant that does not discriminate between the rivals, the claimed dominance is unsubstantiated and fails the requirement to adjudicate between protocols on the evidence.","target":"6a95e6c7a4e9c26e46092d64063756fd1884dc67e54b6d9bbfe6b06367f186b0"},{"attack":true,"case":"The target never adjudicates between rival protocols such as log-based or lease-based alternatives, violating the problem's explicit instruction to compare rivals. Furthermore, by relying on asynchronous publication of Merkle leaves while conditioning safety on a quorum certificate for the subtree root, the protocol permits silent displacement: the holder can crash after the root is witnessed but before all leaf data is durably stored, leaving the quorum certificate intact yet the actual completed evidence irretrievable.","target":"d0c1cb17e452843e6791556aadc0e9397e48b30084ac57dff386ef2d4e4a3e77"}]}